## Part 1 — Serve the store name as the home page heading

**File:** `services/web/server.js`

**Contract:** a `GET /` response must contain exactly one top-level heading element whose text content is the literal string `Corner Shop` — no surrounding whitespace, no nested markup, no extra inline elements inside the `h1`. `page.locator('h1').textContent()` must return `"Corner Shop"` precisely; note the locator resolves strictly, so there must remain exactly one `h1` in the document.

**Intended implementation:** in the `page` template literal, change the heading line from `<h1>Welcome</h1>` to `<h1>Corner Shop</h1>`. Keep the heading's text as a direct, single text node on one line — do not reformat it across lines or indent the text inside the tag, because `textContent()` is compared with `toBe` and would then pick up newlines and spaces and fail again.

If you prefer to remove the duplication between `<title>` and `<h1>`, it is acceptable to introduce a single module-level constant (for example `const storeName = 'Corner Shop';`) and interpolate it into both the `<title>` and the `<h1>`. That is optional; the interpolation must produce byte-identical output to the literal form — in particular no template-literal newline leaks into the `h1`. Keep it simple; a one-word literal edit is sufficient and lowest risk.

**Tests served:** `home.spec.ts > home page > shows the store name as its heading` — the sole member of the failing set. This is the only change required to turn that assertion green.

